    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 80-year-old girl presents with a 1-year history of progressive unilateral nasal obstruction and recurrent epistaxis. Nasal endoscopy reveals a fleshy, cauliflower-like mass originating from the lateral nasal wall. Biopsy confirms an Inverted Papilloma. What is the primary clinical significance of this tumor?', '["Strong association with Epstein-Barr Virus and obligate radiotherapy", "High rate of local recurrence and a 5-10% risk of squamous cell carcinoma transformation", "Congenital connection to the anterior cranial fossa via the foramen cecum", "High risk of hematogenous metastasis to the lungs and bone", "Testosterone-dependent vascular tumor with massive office biopsy bleeding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sinonasal Inverted Papilloma is a benign but locally aggressive epithelial tumor originating from the lateral nasal wall. It has two major clinical hallmarks: a high rate of local recurrence if not fully excised (requires complete surgical excision) and an associated 5-10% risk of synchronous or metachronous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) transformation.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 14-year-old male presents with recurrent, massive, spontaneous left-sided epistaxis and progressive nasal obstruction. Nasal endoscopy reveals a lobulated, reddish-purple mass in the posterior nasal cavity. Imaging shows anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary sinus wall (Holman-Miller sign). What is the most appropriate management strategy?', '["Surgical resection after preoperative embolization; diagnostic office biopsy is contraindicated", "Intranasal corticosteroid sprays and systemic antihistamines", "Radiotherapy as the primary, first-line curative treatment", "Immediate office biopsy to confirm histopathology followed by chemotherapy", "Observation with serial MRI scans until puberty"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a classic presentation of a Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA), a benign but highly vascular, locally invasive tumor occurring almost exclusively in adolescent males. The Holman-Miller sign (anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary wall) is pathognomonic on CT/MRI. Office biopsy is strictly contraindicated due to the extreme risk of catastrophic, uncontrollable hemorrhage. Management is surgical resection preceded by angiographic embolization to reduce blood loss.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old boy of Southern Chinese descent presents with a painless neck lump and unilateral right-sided ear clogging. Examination shows a serous middle ear effusion on the right, and a firm Level II cervical lymph node. Biopsy confirms undifferentiated Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma. What is the primary etiologic agent?', '["Human Papillomavirus (HPV)", "Occupational exposure to hardwood dust", "Chronic sinusitis and allergic rhinitis", "Tobacco and alcohol abuse", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC), particularly the WHO Type III undifferentiated variant, is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) infection. It is highly endemic in Southern China. Unilateral otitis media with effusion (OME) in an adult is a critical red flag and warrants direct fiberoptic examination of the nasopharynx to rule out NPC obstructing the Eustachian tube. Cervical lymphadenopathy (Level II) is the most common presenting symptom.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old lady presents with 11 days of facial pressure, nasal congestion, and yellow nasal discharge. The patient initially felt better on day 5, but symptoms returned with greater severity on day 8 ("double sickening"). Vitals show Temp 100.2°F. What is the most likely pathogen?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Rhinovirus", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Aspergillus fumigatus", "Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical picture of symptoms lasting >10 days or ''double sickening'' (worsening on day 5-8 after initial improvement) suggests Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis (ABRS). Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae are the most common bacterial pathogens. Viral rhinosinusitis typically resolves within 10 days.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old boy is diagnosed with Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis (ABRS). The patient has a history of mild rash after taking penicillin. What is the first-line empiric antibiotic therapy according to current IDSA guidelines?', '["Amoxicillin alone", "Azithromycin", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Clindamycin", "Ciprofloxacin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Current IDSA guidelines recommend Amoxicillin-clavulanate (standard or high-dose) as first-line therapy for ABRS to cover beta-lactamase-producing H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis. For patients with a non-type I penicillin allergy (like a mild rash), third-generation cephalosporins or respiratory fluoroquinolones are alternatives, but Amoxicillin-clavulanate remains first-line for those without severe allergies.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'Which of the following represents a "major" symptom criteria for the diagnosis of acute rhinosinusitis under the AAO-HNS guidelines?', '["Halitosis", "Purulent nasal discharge", "Cough", "Headache", "Ear pain/fullness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Major criteria include: purulent anterior nasal discharge, purulent nasal blockage/obstruction, facial congestion/fullness, facial pain/pressure, and hyposmia/anosmia. Minor criteria include headache, fever, halitosis, fatigue, dental pain, cough, and ear pain/fullness.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old male presents with bilateral nasal obstruction, facial pressure, and post-nasal drip for 5 months. Nasal endoscopy shows mucosal edema and purulent drainage in the middle meatus without polyps. What is the minimum duration of symptoms required to establish a diagnosis of Chronic Rhinosinusitis (CRS)?', '["6 months", "1 year", "12 weeks", "4 weeks", "8 weeks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Chronic Rhinosinusitis (CRS) is defined by at least 12 weeks of continuous inflammatory symptoms (such as mucopurulent drainage, nasal obstruction, facial pain/pressure, or decreased smell) plus objective evidence of sinonasal inflammation on endoscopy or CT scan.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old male is diagnosed with Chronic Rhinosinusitis with Nasal Polyps (CRSwNP) and has a history of severe adult-onset asthma. The patient experiences a severe bronchospastic reaction after taking ibuprofen. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this syndrome?', '["T-cell mediated delayed hypersensitivity", "Type III immune-complex mediated hypersensitivity", "Dysregulation of arachidonic acid metabolism with leukotriene overproduction", "Autoimmune destruction of nasal cartilage", "IgE-mediated hypersensitivity to NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Samter''s Triad (Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease / AERD): nasal polyps, asthma, and aspirin/NSAID sensitivity. The pathophysiology is non-allergic and involves dysregulation of the arachidonic acid pathway, specifically COX-1 inhibition leading to shunting towards the lipoxygenase pathway and massive overproduction of pro-inflammatory cysteinyl leukotrienes.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 50-year-old child is found to have bilateral nasal polyposis on anterior rhinoscopy. Which of the following diagnostic tests is most crucial to perform next in this patient?', '["Serum IgA levels", "Sweat chloride test", "CT scan of the brain", "Biopsy of the polyp", "Skin prick allergy panel"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral nasal polyps are extremely rare in children. In pediatric patients, nasal polyposis is considered pathognomonic for Cystic Fibrosis (CF) until proven otherwise. A sweat chloride test must be performed immediately to rule out CF.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 3-year-old lady with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes presents with severe left-sided facial pain, headache, and orbital swelling. Nasal endoscopy reveals a dry, black eschar on the left middle turbinate. What is the hallmark microscopic finding on tissue biopsy?', '["Broad, ribbon-like, non-septate hyphae branching at 90 degrees", "Budding yeast cells with pseudohyphae", "Capsulated yeast cells showing narrow-based budding", "Narrow, septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees", "Spherules containing thousands of endospores"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical presentation describes Acute Invasive Fungal Sinusitis (Mucormycosis), a highly lethal infection seen in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) or immunocompromised patients. The causative Mucorales fungi invade blood vessels, causing thrombosis and tissue necrosis (black eschar). Biopsy characteristically shows broad, non-septate hyphae branching at right (90-degree) angles.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old female with acute myeloid leukemia and severe neutropenia develops fever, facial pain, and progressive unilateral vision loss. Endoscopy reveals ischemic mucosal necrosis. Biopsy shows narrow, septate hyphae branching at 45-degree angles. What is the most appropriate first-line medical treatment?', '["High-dose oral Fluconazole", "Intravenous Amphotericin B and urgent surgical debridement", "Oral Voriconazole alone", "Intravenous Caspofungin and broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Hyperbaric oxygen and topical corticosteroid sprays"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Acute Invasive Fungal Sinusitis due to Aspergillus (characterized by narrow septate hyphae branching at 45-degree angles). It is a surgical emergency requiring immediate, aggressive surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue combined with systemic antieukaryotic therapy (IV Amphotericin B or Voriconazole). Delay leads to intracranial spread and death.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 25-year-old lady presents with progressive unilateral nasal obstruction and mild proptosis. CT scan shows heterogeneous opacification of the maxillary sinus with characteristic central "hyperdense" signals. During surgery, a thick, "peanut butter-like" mucin is evacuated. Pathology reveals Charcot-Leyden crystals and rare non-invasive fungal hyphae. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acute Invasive Fungal Sinusitis", "Antrochoanal Polyp", "Allergic Fungal Rhinosinusitis (AFRS)", "Chronic Granulomatous Fungal Sinusitis", "Maxillary Sinus Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AFRS is a non-invasive, IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reaction to colonizing fungi (usually Dematiaceous fungi like Bipolaris or Alternaria). Classic findings include hyperdense signals on CT (due to accumulated fungal heavy metals and calcium), thick ''peanut butter'' allergic mucin containing Charcot-Leyden crystals (degraded eosinophils), and fungal elements without tissue invasion.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 8-year-old woman with a recent ethmoid sinusitis presents with left eye swelling, painful extraocular movements, proptosis, and decreased visual acuity. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Subperiosteal abscess without orbital involvement", "Preseptal cellulitis (Chandler Stage I)", "Orbital cellulitis (Chandler Stage II/III)", "Cavernous sinus thrombosis (Chandler Stage V)", "Thyroid eye disease"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Orbital cellulitis (Chandler Stage II) involves infection spreading deep to the orbital septum (usually from the ethmoid sinus via the lamina papyracea). It is distinguished from preseptal cellulitis by the presence of proptosis, ophthalmoplegia (painful/restricted eye movements), and potentially decreased vision.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 45-year-old female presents with a tender, fluctuant, soft-tissue swelling over the forehead following a 2-week history of untreated frontal sinusitis. CT scan reveals frontal bone osteomyelitis with an associated subperiosteal abscess. What is this clinical entity called?', '["Silent Sinus Syndrome", "Wegener''s granulomatosis", "Mucoceles of the frontal sinus", "Pott''s Puffy Tumor", "Brown''s tumor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pott''s Puffy Tumor is a rare but classic complication of frontal sinusitis characterized by osteomyelitis of the frontal bone and a forehead subperiosteal abscess. It spreads via thrombophlebitis of the diploic veins and carries a high risk of intracranial spread (e.g., epidural or subdural empyema).'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old girl with severe sphenoid sinusitis develops high fever, bilateral eye swelling, severe chemosis, progressive proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia with loss of the corneal reflex. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Bilateral orbital cellulitis", "Pituitary apoplexy", "Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis", "Subperiosteal abscess", "Thyroid storm"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis (CST) is a life-threatening complication typically arising from sphenoid or ethmoid sinusitis (or facial infections in the danger triangle). It is characterized by high fever, rapid progression to bilateral orbital symptoms (proptosis, chemosis) due to venous engorgement, and cranial nerve palsies (CN III, IV, V1/V2, VI) due to compression within the cavernous sinus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 60-year-old boy presents with active bleeding from the anterior right nasal septum. The clinician identifies Kiesselbach''s plexus as the source. Which of the following arteries does NOT contribute to this vascular plexus?', '["Anterior ethmoidal artery", "Greater palatine artery", "Superior labial artery", "Sphenopalatine artery", "Posterior ethmoidal artery"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Kiesselbach''s plexus (Little''s area) on the anterior nasal septum is the site of over 90% of nosebleeds. It is an anastomosis of 4-5 arteries: Anterior ethmoidal (ophthalmic a. branch), Sphenopalatine (maxillary a. branch), Greater palatine (maxillary a. branch), and Superior labial (facial a. branch). The posterior ethmoidal artery does NOT contribute to this plexus.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old female presents with severe epistaxis that streams down the posterior pharynx. Pressure and anterior packing fail to stop the bleeding. The patient is admitted to the ICU with posterior packing. What is the primary arterial supply to the region of Woodruff''s plexus?', '["Sphenopalatine artery", "Facial artery", "Superior labial artery", "Internal carotid artery", "Anterior ethmoidal artery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Posterior epistaxis arises from Woodruff''s plexus, located posterior to the inferior turbinate. The primary blood supply is the sphenopalatine artery (a terminal branch of the maxillary artery / external carotid system). Posterior bleeds are severe, arterial, and can lead to hypoxia or bradycardia (nasopulmonary reflex) when packed, requiring close monitoring.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old girl presents with lifelong recurrent nosebleeds, and their parent has a similar history. Examination reveals multiple red, blanching telangiectasias on the nasal mucosa, lips, and tongue. What is the inheritance pattern of this disease?', '["Autosomal dominant", "X-linked recessive", "X-linked dominant", "Autosomal recessive", "Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia (HHT / Osler-Weber-Rendu disease). It is an autosomal dominant vascular dysplasia characterized by recurrent epistaxis, cutaneous/mucosal telangiectasias, and visceral arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) in the lungs, liver, and brain.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 40-year-old male complains of sneezing, itchy nose, and watery eyes every spring. Nasal endoscopy reveals pale, boggy, bluish-gray turbinates. What is the first-line, most effective long-term medical therapy for this condition?', '["Oral H1-receptor antihistamine", "Oral leukotriene receptor antagonist", "Intranasal saline irrigation", "Topical nasal decongestant (Oxymetazoline)", "Intranasal corticosteroid spray"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Allergic Rhinitis (IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity). Intranasal corticosteroids (e.g., Fluticasone, Budesonide) are the most effective first-line monotherapy for moderate-to-severe symptoms, superior to oral antihistamines, especially for nasal congestion.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old woman experiences profuse watery rhinorrhea when moving from a warm room into cold air or when eating hot, spicy foods. Allergy testing is completely negative. What is the first-line topical treatment for this patient''s primary symptom?', '["Intranasal Azelastine", "Topical Oxymetazoline", "Intranasal Ipratropium bromide", "Oral Pseudoephedrine", "Intranasal Fluticasone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Vasomotor Rhinitis (Non-allergic rhinitis), characterized by nasal hyper-responsiveness to physical triggers (temperature, dry air, food). The profuse watery rhinorrhea is parasympathetically driven. Intranasal Ipratropium bromide, a topical anticholinergic agent, is highly effective at blocking these secretomotor pathways.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old gentleman presents with severe, refractory nasal congestion. The patient has been using an over-the-counter oxymetazoline nasal spray 5-6 times daily for 3 weeks. Examination shows beefy, red, and edematous nasal mucosa. What is the pathophysiological cause of this condition?', '["Bacterial colonization of the nasal mucosa", "Beta-adrenergic receptor hypersensitivity", "Th2-mediated eosinophilic inflammation", "Parasympathetic secretomotor blockade", "Downregulation and desensitization of alpha-adrenergic receptors"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This describes Rhinitis Medicamentosa, a rebound nasal congestion resulting from prolonged use (>3-5 days) of topical nasal decongestants (like oxymetazoline). Constant receptor stimulation causes downregulation and desensitization of local alpha-adrenergic receptors, leading to severe rebound vasodilation when the drug wears off.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 18-year-old child is brought to the clinic with unilateral, foul-smelling, purulent nasal discharge for 3 days. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Nasal dermoid cyst", "Allergic rhinitis", "Acute maxillary sinusitis", "Nasal foreign body", "Choanal atresia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a pediatric patient, unilateral, foul-smelling, purulent or bloody nasal discharge is considered a nasal foreign body until proven otherwise. Immediate inspection and removal are indicated.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 55-year-old child is brought to the emergency department after placing a button battery in the left nostril 1 hour ago. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Apply silver nitrate cautery to the surrounding mucosa", "Nasal irrigation with normal saline to flush the battery out", "Prescribe oral antibiotics and topical steroids for discharge", "Observation for 24 hours to see if it passes into the nasopharynx", "Urgent endoscopic removal under direct visualization and avoidance of irrigation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A button battery in the nose is a true medical emergency. It causes rapid tissue destruction via liquefactive necrosis, electrolysis, and chemical burns within 2 hours, leading to septal perforation. Immediate, urgent removal is required. Saline or water irrigation is strictly contraindicated as it accelerates the electrical reaction.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 70-year-old male is evaluated in the clinic 2 hours after a nasal injury. The patient has complete nasal obstruction. Examination shows a symmetric, fluctuant, dark-purple bulge along both sides of the anterior nasal septum. What is the most urgent next step?', '["Urgent CT scan of the facial bones", "Cold compresses and referral to ENT in 1 week", "Closed reduction of the nasal bones under local anesthesia", "Oral antibiotics and close outpatient follow-up", "Immediate incision and drainage followed by bilateral anterior nasal packing"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has a Septal Hematoma. Because the septal cartilage relies entirely on the overlying perichondrium for blood supply, a hematoma cuts off this perfusion. Untreated, ischemic cartilage necrosis occurs within hours, leading to septal collapse, septal abscess, and a permanent ''saddle nose deformity''. Immediate I&D and packing are mandatory.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 20-year-old man presents with clear, watery fluid leaking from the left nostril after a motor vehicle collision. The fluid tests positive for Beta-2 transferrin. What is the source of this fluid?', '["Allergic serous secretion", "Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)", "Sphenoid sinus cyst fluid", "Maxillary sinus mucocele", "Lacrimal gland duct overflow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A clear, watery nasal discharge following head trauma that increases when leaning forward is highly suggestive of a CSF leak (CSF rhinorrhea). The gold-standard laboratory test to confirm CSF is the detection of Beta-2 transferrin, a protein found uniquely in CSF, perilymph, and vitreous humor, but absent in normal nasal secretions.'),
    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 35-year-old man presents with a 1-year history of progressive unilateral nasal obstruction and recurrent epistaxis. Nasal endoscopy reveals a fleshy, cauliflower-like mass originating from the lateral nasal wall. Biopsy confirms an Inverted Papilloma. What is the primary clinical significance of this tumor?', '["Strong association with Epstein-Barr Virus and obligate radiotherapy", "High rate of local recurrence and a 5-10% risk of squamous cell carcinoma transformation", "Testosterone-dependent vascular tumor with massive office biopsy bleeding", "Congenital connection to the anterior cranial fossa via the foramen cecum", "High risk of hematogenous metastasis to the lungs and bone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sinonasal Inverted Papilloma is a benign but locally aggressive epithelial tumor originating from the lateral nasal wall. It has two major clinical hallmarks: a high rate of local recurrence if not fully excised (requires complete surgical excision) and an associated 5-10% risk of synchronous or metachronous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) transformation.'),
