    ((SELECT id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'ent-nose' LIMIT 1), 'A 75-year-old male with severe sphenoid sinusitis develops high fever, bilateral eye swelling, severe chemosis, progressive proptosis, and ophthalmoplegia with loss of the corneal reflex. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Subperiosteal abscess", "Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis", "Bilateral orbital cellulitis", "Pituitary apoplexy", "Thyroid storm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis (CST) is a life-threatening complication typically arising from sphenoid or ethmoid sinusitis (or facial infections in the danger triangle). It is characterized by high fever, rapid progression to bilateral orbital symptoms (proptosis, chemosis) due to venous engorgement, and cranial nerve palsies (CN III, IV, V1/V2, VI) due to compression within the cavernous sinus.'),
